# Globbed, not exact: a keys.env.bak is exactly as sensitive as keys.env, and
# taking a .bak before editing the file is a normal thing to do (the VPS now has
# one). An exact-match rule would have left that copy trackable.
keys.env*
apikey.txt*
# Signs dashboard session cookies. Same trust boundary as the keys above:
# whoever holds it can forge a logged-in session.
server/session_secret.txt
node_modules/
*.log
__pycache__/
*.pyc
tasks/logs/
tasks/backups/
vps-backups/
tasks/.tv_session.json
# Per-machine, per-account circuit breaker counters. Live runtime state: tracking
# it would let a git pull overwrite one machine's loss streak with another's.
tasks/breaker_state_*.json
tasks/*.pine

# Exported MT5 bars — large and regenerable via tasks/export_mt5_history.py
tasks/history/

# Analysis output — ~400KB per run (report + fact pack + analyst replies) and
# fully regenerable by parallel_analysis.py. The server reads latest.json from
# disk, so nothing depends on these being in git.
tasks/analysis/

# Per-machine live state: a halt is a local safety decision and must never
# deploy from one box to another, and the VPS keeps its own copy untracked.
server/trading_control.json

# Per-machine live state. strategy_settings.json in particular must stay untracked:
# it carries fixedLotSize, and a shared commit would push this machine's position
# sizing onto the VPS. A BOM in this file already reset VPS sizing once (2026-08-02).
server/strategy_settings.json
server/journal.json

# The TradingView alert feed and its append-only archive. Per-machine live state,
# same as journal.json: each box receives its own webhooks, and a shared commit
# would splice one box's alert history into the other's. Untracked, NOT
# unimportant — tv_alerts.jsonl is never trimmed and is the record that survives
# the 50-entry display cap.
server/tv_alerts.json
tasks/logs/tv_alerts.jsonl

# Derived from each box's own rejection ledger by tasks/learning_from_rejections.py.
# Regenerable, per-machine, and would conflict on every sync if tracked.
server/learning_shadow.json
tasks/ai_brief.md

# Auto-healer backups of learning.json / journal.json. Live data copies,
# rewritten every time the healer confirms the source is valid — not history.
*.json.bak

# Built design-canvas payload (2.2MB). Regenerated from design/*.dc.html — see design/README.md
design/*.html
!design/*.dc.html

# Per-machine near-miss evidence, appended by server/near_miss.js flushNearMisses().
# Runtime data like tasks/rejections.jsonl, NOT code: each box records its own bars and
# its own ceiling, so committing it makes the two boxes conflict on every sync. Ignored
# rather than merely untracked because the standing rule is to commit immediately after
# every edit, and one `git add -A` would otherwise sweep it in.
tasks/near_misses.jsonl

# Per-machine stop-variant shadow rows, appended by server/stop_variants.js. Same
# reasoning as the line above: each box shadows its own signals against its own bars.
tasks/stop_variants.jsonl

# The rest of the rejection-ledger family, and the per-machine session state.
#
# The comment above has cited tasks/rejections.jsonl as THE example of this category
# since it was written, and the rule never covered it — the file it names was the one
# file it did not ignore. That is the same shape as CLAUDE.md telling every session to
# call GET /api/performance while another line in the same file said the route does not
# exist: documentation stating a rule the enforcement never implemented.
#
# It matters for the reason the comment already gives. These are appended per box from
# that box's own broker bars, so committing them makes the laptop and the VPS conflict
# on every sync, and the standing rule to commit after every edit means one `git add -A`
# does exactly that. None of these is tracked today, so nothing already in the repo is
# hidden by adding them.
tasks/rejections.jsonl
tasks/rejections_scored.jsonl
tasks/rr_rejected.jsonl
tasks/shadow_shorts.jsonl
tasks/shadow_shorts_scored.jsonl
tasks/agent_queue.jsonl

# Written by tasks/hooks/session-stop.ps1 with this box's last commits and dirty files.
tasks/jarvis-state.json

# Regenerable Yahoo bar cache for tasks/instrument_universe_scan.cjs
tasks/history_yahoo/

# Timestamped pre-edit backups: <file>.bak-<label>-<timestamp>.
#
# NOT a cleanup rule. Rule 4 says copy before you rewrite, and these are that rule
# working -- roughly 90 of them, and every one stays exactly where it is. Nothing here
# deletes anything; the files simply stop being reported as untracked.
#
# The reason is signal, not tidiness. `git status` had grown to ~190 lines with about
# half of it backups, and the standing rule "never leave server/index.js with
# uncommitted changes" cannot be checked against output nobody can read. A real
# modification to a trading file was indistinguishable from backup noise.
#
# `*.json.bak` above catches an older convention and matches none of these, which is
# why they accumulated in the first place.
*.bak-*
*.bak-*/

# EXCEPT tasks/snapshots/ — four TRACKED files match the pattern above:
#   tasks/snapshots/.bak-20260829_105937/{gate-health,performance,signals}.schema.json
#   tasks/snapshots/signals.schema.json.bak-premacro-20260831_103027
# They are the API-shape baselines api_snapshot.cjs compares against, and that check
# runs from post-edit-check on every server/index.js edit. Git does not untrack a file
# because of a new ignore rule, so they were never actually at risk — this negation
# exists so they cannot become at risk later, in a fresh clone or after a stray
# `git rm --cached`. A regression baseline that silently stops being compared is the
# exact shape of the failure 156 commits in this repo describe as "silently".
!tasks/snapshots/
!tasks/snapshots/**
tasks/calendar_store.json
tasks/calendar_store.json.tmp

# ── Run markers, 2026-09-06 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Same argument as the *.bak-* block above: signal, not tidiness. These are ZERO-BYTE
# "did this already run today" flags written by auto_daily.bat and morning_ready.ps1,
# one per calendar day, forever. 21 of them were sitting in `git status` untracked,
# and the startup hook reports the raw count as "147 uncommitted file(s) -- commit
# before editing", so a real edit to a trading file was buried in day markers.
#
# NOTHING IS DELETED and nothing is untracked. tasks/.daily_ran_20260728 and _20260730
# were committed before this rule existed; git does not untrack a file because of a
# new ignore line, so those two stay tracked and stay in history. (Both had in fact
# been DELETED from the working tree and were restored on 2026-09-06 rather than
# having the deletion committed -- rule 6 has no exceptions, including for a
# zero-byte file whose only content is its own name.)
tasks/.daily_ran_*
tasks/.morning_ready_*
tasks/.auto_runner_*

# Scratch files left by interrupted sessions. Kept on disk under rule 6, never tracked.
*.junk-*

# ── Generated stores, 2026-09-06 ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# A `git add -A` on 2026-09-06 swept these into a commit: 851 files, 1.47M
# insertions, including a 32MB chroma.sqlite3. The commit was reset the same
# minute -- nothing was pushed and no file was touched on disk -- but it is
# exactly the bloat the *.bak-* block above exists to prevent, so the rule is
# written down rather than left to the next `git add -A` to rediscover.
#
# tasks/rag_db/ is a chromadb vector store, 41MB and rewritten on every
# `python tasks/rag_index.py`. It is DERIVED: the source of truth is the 348
# memory files it indexes, and a rebuild takes seconds. Tracking a binary blob
# that is regenerated from tracked-elsewhere text buys nothing and makes every
# future diff unreadable.
#
# .tmp_daily/ holds scratch copies of server/index.js written during VPS parity
# proofs. Seven near-identical 10k-line copies of the engine is the single worst
# thing that could be in a diff you are trying to read for a real change.
tasks/rag_db/
.tmp_daily/
